# Lock-Free Programming

## What is Lock-Free Programming?

Lock-free algorithms allow multiple threads to operate on shared data without relying on traditional blocking mutexes.

A common technique is CAS.

```text
READ
 ↓
MODIFY
 ↓
CAS
 ↓
Success?
 ├── YES → Done
 └── NO  → Retry
```

## Why Use Lock-Free Algorithms?

Potential benefits include:

* Avoiding blocking
* Reducing lock contention
* High throughput
* Predictable progress properties

They are especially interesting in high-performance concurrent systems.

## Lock-Free Does Not Mean "No Synchronization"

Lock-free algorithms still use synchronization.

They commonly rely on:

```text
Atomics
+
CAS
+
Memory ordering
```

## Progress Guarantees

### Obstruction-Free

A thread can make progress if it eventually runs without interference.

### Lock-Free

The system as a whole always makes progress.

At least one operation completes within a finite number of steps.

### Wait-Free

Every operation completes within a bounded number of steps.

```text
Obstruction-free
      ↓
Lock-free
      ↓
Wait-free
```

These guarantees should not be confused.

## Difficult Problems

Lock-free programming requires understanding:

* CAS
* ABA
* Memory ordering
* Memory reclamation
* Cache coherence
* Object lifetime

## Common Mistake

Do not assume:

```text
lock-free = automatically faster
```

A lock-free algorithm can be slower because of:

* CAS retries
* Cache contention
* Memory allocation
* Poor memory ordering
* Complex memory reclamation

